"A particle is projected upwards from the ground with an initial velocity of \( 20 \, \text{m/s} \). Assuming the acceleration due to gravity is \( 9.8 \, \text{m/s}^2 \) downward and neglecting air resistance, find the maximum height reached by the particle.","<bos><start_of_turn>user
A particle is projected upwards from the ground with an initial velocity of \( 20 \, \text{m/s} \). Assuming the acceleration due to gravity is \( 9.8 \, \text{m/s}^2 \) downward and neglecting air resistance, find the maximum height reached by the particle.<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To find the maximum height reached by the particle, we use the kinematic equations for motion under constant acceleration. The initial velocity \( u = 20 \, \text{m/s} \), the final velocity at the maximum height \( v = 0 \, \text{m/s} \), and the acceleration due to gravity \( a = -9.8 \, \text{m/s}^2 \) (since it is downward). The height \( h \) is the maximum height.

Using the kinematic equation:
\[
v^2 = u^2 + 2as
\]
Substituting the given values:
\[
0^2 = (20)^2 + 2(-9.8)h
\]
Solving for \( h \):
\[
0 = 400 - 19.6h
\]
\[
19.6h = 400
\]
\[
h = \frac{400}{19.6} \approx 20.408 \, \text{m}
\]
Thus, the maximum height reached by the particle is approximately \(\boxed{20.41 \, \text{m}}\).",relevance,"# Task Description
