","To find the length of the train, we use the formula:
\[
\text{Time} = \frac{\text{Distance}}{\text{Speed}}
\]
Given:
- Joger's speed = 9 km/hr = \(9 \times \frac{1000}{3600}\) = 25 m/s
- Train's speed = 45 km/hr = \(45 \times \frac{1000}{3600}\) = 12.5 m/s
- Distance between joger and train = 150 m
- Time taken for train to pass the joger = 25 s

The train's speed relative to the joger is:
\[
\text{Relative speed} = \text{Train's speed} - \text{Joguer's speed} = 12.5 \text{ m/s} - 25 \text{ m/s} = 12.5 \text{ m/s}
\]
The relative speed is:
\[
\text{Relative speed} = 12.5 \text{ m/s} - 25 \text{ m/s} = 12.5 \text{ m/s}
\]
The correct relative speed is:
\[
\text{Relative speed} = 45 \text{ km/hr} - 9 \text{ km/hr} = 36 \text{ km/hr} = 36 \times \frac{1000}{3600} = 10 \text{ m/s}
\]
The time taken for the train to pass the joguer is 25 s. During this time, the train must cover the distance of 150 m (initial distance) plus the length of the train. Let \(l\) be the length of the train. Then:
\[
\text{Distance} = \text{Relative speed} \times \text{Time}
\]
\[
150 + l = 10 \times 25
\]
\[
150 + l = 250
\]
\[
l = 250 - 150 = 100 \text{ m}
\]
