","To prove that \( U(r, \theta) \) is harmonic inside the circle \( |z| = 1 \), we proceed by first showing that \( U(r, \theta) \) is a function of the complex variable \( z = r \cos \theta + ir \sin \theta \).

Substitute \( r = |z| \) and \( \theta = \arg z \) into the function \( U(r, \theta) \):
\[
U(z) = \frac{2}{\pi} \tan^{-1} \left( \frac{2z |z| \sin \theta}{1 - |z|^2} \right) = \frac{2}{\pi} \tan^{-1} \left( \frac{2z |z] \sin \theta}{1 - |z|^2} \right).
\]
Since \( z = r \cos \theta + ir \sin \theta \), we have \( z = r \cos \theta + ir \sin \theta \), so \( z = r \cos \theta + ir \sin \theta \).

The function \( U(r, \theta) \) is given by:
\[
U(r, \theta) = \frac{2}{\pi} \tan^{-1} \left( \frac{2r \sin \theta}{1 - r^2} \right).
\]
Let \( z = r \cos \theta + ir \sin \theta \). Then \( r = |z| \) and \( \theta = \arg z \). Substituting these into \( U(r, \theta) \), we get:
\[
U(z) = \frac{2}{\pi} \tan^{-1} \left( \frac{2|z| \sin \theta}{1 - |z|^2} \right).
\]
However, this is not a function of \( z \) alone, as it depends on both \( |z| \) and \( \arg z \). 

