A proton with mass \(1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}\) and charge \(1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}\) accelerates from rest in a uniform electric field of strength \(500 \, \text{N/C}\).  
(a) What is the magnitude of the acceleration of the proton?  
(b) How long does it take the proton to reach a speed of \(35,000 \, \text{m/s}\)?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
(a) The acceleration of the proton is given by the force on the proton divided by its mass. The force on the proton is given by the product of the charge and the electric field:  
\[
F = qE = (1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}) (500 \, \text{N/C}) = 8.0 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}.
\]  
The acceleration is then:  
\[
a = \frac{F}{m} = \frac{8.0 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 4.8 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2.
\]  
Thus, the magnitude of the acceleration is \(\boxed{4.8 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2}\).

(b) The time it takes to reach a speed of \(35,000 \, \text{m/s}\) is given by the kinematic equation:  
\[
v = v_0 + at,
\]  
where \(v = 35,000 \, \text{m/s}\), \(v_0 = 0 \, \text{m/s}\) (since the proton starts from rest), and \(a = 4.8 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2\). Solving for \(t\):  
\[
t = \frac{v - v_0}{a} = \frac{35,000 \, \text{m/s} - 0 \, \text{m/s}}{4.8 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2} = 7.2 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{s}.
\]  
Thus, the time it takes is \(\boxed{7.2 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{s}}\).
