","The covariant derivative of a vector \( V^\mu \) in curved spacetime is given by:
\[
\nabla V^\mu = \frac{\partial V^\mu}{\partial x^\mu} + \frac{\partial g^{\mu\mu'}}{\partial x^\mu} \frac{\partial x^\mu}{\partial x^\mu'} V^{\mu'}
\]
where \( \partial_x^\mu \) is the ordinary derivative with respect to the spatial coordinates \( x^\mu \), and \( g^{\mu\mu'} \) is the inverse metric tensor. This expression is often written as:
\[
\nabla V^\mu = \frac{\partial V^\mu}{\partial x^\mu} + g^{\mu\mu'} \frac{\partial g^{\mu\mu'}}{\partial x^\mu} V^{\mu'}
\]
Here, \( \delta_i \) is the Kronecker delta, and \( \delta_{ij} \) is the Kronecker delta in the context of the metric \( g_{ij} \). 

This expression is also often written in terms of the metric \( g_{ij} \) and its components \( g_{ij} \) and \( g_{ij} \) as:
\[
\nabla V^\mu = \frac{\partial V^\mu}{\partial x^\mu} + g_{ij} \frac{\partial g_{ij}}{\partial x^\mu} V^\mu
\]
