The term \( \frac{\partial V^\mu}{\partial x^\mu} \) is the ordinary derivative of \( V^\mu \) with respect to the spatial coordinates \( x^\mu \). This term is valid in flat spacetime, where the metric \( g_{ij} \) is constant. However, in curved spacetime, the metric \( g_{mu\mu'} \) can vary with spacetime coordinates, requiring the additional term to ensure the derivative is well-defined and preserves the conservation of energy-energy and energy-momentum tensors. 

The second term \( g^{\mu\mu'} \frac{\partial V^\mu}{\partial x^\mu'} \) accounts for the change in the metric \( g_{ij} \) with respect to the spatial coordinates \( x^\mu \). This term is called the **Lie derivative** of \( V^\mu \) with respect to the metric \( g_{ij} \), and it accounts for the change in the metric \( g_{ij} \) with respect to the spatial coordinates \( x^\mu \). This term is essential for defining the covariant derivative in curved spacetime, as it ensures that the derivative of a vector is well-defined and preserves the conservation of energy-energy and energy-momentum tensors. 

