An amplifier has a voltage gain \( A_v = 100 \) without feedback. A fraction \( m_v = 0.1 \) of the output is fed back in opposition to the input (negative feedback). If the gain \( A_v \) falls by 6 dB due to ageing, calculate the percentage change in the closed-loop gain \( A_{vf} \).
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The closed-loop voltage gain \( A_{vf} \) is given by:
\[
A_{vf} = \frac{A_v}{1 - m_v}
\]
Substituting the given values:
\[
A_{vf} = \frac{100}{1 - 0.1} = \frac{100}{0.9} \approx 111.11 \text{ V}
\]
The gain \( A_v \) decreases by 6 dB due to ageing. The new gain \( A_v' \) is:
\[
A_v' = \frac{A_v}{10^{6/10}} = \frac{100}{10^{0.6}} \approx 63.1 \text{ V}
\]
The new closed-loop voltage gain \( A_{vf}' \) is:
\[
A_{vf}' = \frac{A_v'}{1 - m_v} = \frac{63.1}{1 - 0.1} = \frac{63.1}{0.9} \approx 70.11 \text{ V}
\]
The percentage change in the closed-loop gain is:
\[
\text{Percentage change} = \left( \frac{A_{vf}' - A_{vf}}{A_{vf}} \right) \times 100 = \left( \frac{70.11 - 111.11}{111.11} \right) \times 100 \approx -36.1\%
\]
Thus, the percentage change in the closed-loop gain is \(\boxed{-36.1\%}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

