<USER_REQUEST>
A spaceship is moving at a speed of 0.6c relative to Earth. Two clocks are on board the ship - one in the cockpit and one in the cargo bay. If the clock in the cockpit shows a time of 3 years when the ship returns to Earth, what will be the time shown on the clock in the cargo bay when it is compared to a clock on Earth? Use the concept of time dilation to calculate the answer.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The time dilation formula is given by:

\[
t = \frac{t_0}{\sqrt{1 - \beta^2}}
\]

where:
- \( t \) is the time measured by the observer in the Earth frame,
- \( t_0 \) is the time measured by the object in motion (the clock in the cockpit),
- \( \beta \) is the speed of the object in motion relative to the observer, expressed as \( \beta = \frac{v}{c} \), where \( v \) is the velocity and \( c \) is the speed of light.

Given:
- \( t_0 = 3 \) years (time in the spacecraft's frame)
- \( \beta = 0.6 \)

First, calculate the time \( t \) in the Earth frame:

\[
t = \frac{3}{\sqrt{1 - (0.6)^2}} = \frac{3}{\sqrt{1 - 0.36}} = \frac{3}{\sqrt{0.64}} = \frac{3}{0.8} = 3.75 \text{ years}
\]

Thus, the clock on Earth will show 3.75 years when the clock in the cockpit shows 3 years.

The time difference between the Earth clock and the clock in the carriage is:
\[
3.75 - 3 = 0.75 \text{ years}
\]

Since the clock in the cargo board is in the same frame as the clock in the cockpit, the time difference between the two clocks is negligible. Thus, the time on the clock in the cargo bar will also be 3 years.
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

