","The net chemical reaction for the discharging of a lead storage battery is:  
\[ \text{Pb} + \text{PbSO}_4 + \text{H}_2\text{SO}_4 \rightarrow \text{PbSO}_4 + \text{PbSO}_4 + \text{H}_2\text{O} \]  
This is a simplified version, as the reaction occurs in two half-cells. The correct net reaction is:  
\[ \text{Pb} + \text{PbSO}_4 + 2\text{H}^+ + \text{SO}_4^{2-} \rightarrow \text{PbSO}_4 + \text{Pb}^{2+} + \text{H}_2\text{O} \]  

**Explanation:**  
- The anode (left side) is pure lead (Pb), which is oxidized to lead ions (Pb²⁺).  
- The cathode (right side) is lead dioxide (PbO₂), which is reduced to lead (Pb).  
- Both half-cells involve the reduction of sulfate ions (SO₄²⁻) to sulfate ions (SO₃²⁻), which combine with the oxidized lead to form lead sulfate (PbSO₄).  
- The overall reaction consumes sulfuric acid (H₂SO₄) and produces water (H₂O).  

Thus, the net result is the formation of lead sulfate (PbSO₄) on both the anode and cathode, while the sulfuric acid is consumed.  

\[ \boxed{\text{Pb} + \text{PbSO}_4 + \text{H}_2\text{SO}_4 \rightarrow \text{PbSO}_4 + \text{PbSO}_4 + \text{H}_2\text{O}} \]",fluency,"# Task Description
